      "text": "To get to the root of this math problem, we need to make use of the rules sometimes called the Division Rules or the Division Algorithm. These rules tell us:\n\nDividend ÷ Divisor = Quotient + Remainder\n\nWith Divisibility, the Division Rules and the Division Algorithm come in handy with this math problem (and others!). Rather than stick to these names, however, I am going to replace the words \"divisor\" and \"dividend\" with the words \"divided by\" and \"dividends\".\nTo solve this problem, we are going to follow the procedure below to calculate for the greatest three-digit number that is divisible by 6.\n  \n• Finding the first number divisible by 3 - The first number we need is divisible by 3, so let's use the first three-digit multiple of 3, which is 102.  \n• Dividing the first number divisible by 3 by 6 and researching out the quotients - With the first number divisible by 3, 102, let's divide it by 6, giving us that the quotient is 17.  \n• Re-searching out the multiples of 6 that will also be common to the original number - We already know the first number that is divisible by both 3 and 6 when divided by 6 is 6. We quick check 12, 18, and 24. It is logical to understand that 30 and above will not be divisible by both 6 and 3.  \n• Synthesizing the results into answer - We have finally found the first three digits that is divisible by 3 and by 6. It is 186 which is the product of these quotients that we've figured out above.  \nThus, the greatest three-digit number that is divisible by both 3 and 6 is 186. With the help of the rules above, just follow the procedure and solve this and other division problems now!  \n\nAnswer: \\boxed{186}",
